  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 227 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nWesterly flow in the low levels originating from the Gulf of\nAmerica ahead of the approaching upper low moving into northern\nLouisiana should help to bring more moist and warmer air into the\narea. Some low level cumulus will likely develop, but looks \noverall like mostly sunny to partly cloudy conditions primarily.\nHighs on Tuesday with abundant sunshine and weak warm air\nadvection upstream should climb back into the upper 60s to lower\n70s. \n\nThis overall slow warming trend should continue through Wednesday,\nas the weakening upper low swings through the area. Expect\nprimarily on and off partly cloudy conditions (with some very\nbrief mostly cloudy conditions possible on Wednesday. Daytime\nhighs shouldn't moderate much and climb back into the upper 60s to\nlower 70s. No precipitation is expected at this time given the dry\nboundary layer expected. \n\nAs a series of longwave trough axes move SSE through the upper \nlevel flow from the central Great Lakes region Wednesday night\nthrough Friday night. This will bring some showers south through \nnorthern Alabama Wednesday night into Thursday morning. For kept\nrain chances very low (20-35%). However, these rain chances could\ngo up as we get closer to that period. Accumulations will be very\nlight though, less than 0.20 inches in most guidance.\n\nMuch colder air will shift SSE with a strong surface high pushing\nsouthward from central Canada behind this precipitation Thursday\ninto Thursday evening into northern Alabama and southern middle\nTennessee. Highs are currently forecast to only climb into the\nupper 50s to lower 60s. Given the strength of cold air advection\nhinted at, guidance may not have caught up with how colder it will\nreally be yet. Lows drop back into the lower to mid 30s again\nThursday night in lower elevations. However, in higher elevations\nwith a strong pressure gradient (very breezy conditions), lows\nshould be warmer only dropping into the upper 30s to lower 40s.\nSome freezing temperatures might occur, but overall guidance\nsuggests it might be more isolated in nature. Partly cloudy to\nmostly cloudy conditions will likely linger through Friday night,\nbefore decreasing significantly during the day on Saturday. Highs\non Friday look to only reach the lower to mid 50s with continued\ncold air advection and cloud cover. Some models including the NBM\nclear things out though Friday night. If that happens, the colder\nNBM temperatures look on track and we may have a widespread hard\nfreeze for much of the area. \n\nDuring the day on Saturday into Sunday, an amplified upper ridge\nmay try to start building into the area. This will likely lead to\nsunny conditions and some slightly warmer temperatures.",
